# Files that live in this directory because tooling puts them here, but that are
# not public. Wrangler reads this at the root of the uploaded asset directory
# (`dist/public`, per `wrangler.jsonc`) and skips anything it matches; the syntax
# is `.gitignore`'s. It takes effect at upload, not at build — both files below
# are still copied into `dist/public`, they just stop being deployed.
#
# `data/world-mvt/<release>/manifest.json` is the exhaustive tile inventory the
# offline build emits: 8,672,388 bytes naming all 459,493 tile keys. It is the
# *input* `scripts/build-world-manifest.mjs` reads to derive the 13 KB
# `data/world-mvt/manifest.json` the client actually fetches. Nothing requests
# the big one — the client reads the derived manifest, and tiles come out of
# `packed/**` through the Worker — but everything under `client/public/` is
# deployed verbatim, so it was being served anyway: 8.27 MiB behind the
# `/data/world-mvt/:release/*` rule in `_headers`, which grants it
# `max-age=31536000, immutable`, one path segment from the manifest that is real.
#
# Wildcarded rather than named, so a release bump cannot silently start shipping
# it again. `*` does not cross `/` in this syntax, which is what keeps the pattern
# off the derived `data/world-mvt/manifest.json` one segment up, and off all 9,688
# `packed/**` shards and part indexes the Worker serves tiles from via
# `env.ASSETS.fetch` (worker.ts:1378, :1410).
data/world-mvt/*/manifest.json

# `__manus__/debug-collector.js` is the 25,989-byte browser log collector that
# `vitePluginManusDebugCollector` injects — and that plugin only runs when
# `command === "serve"` (vite.config.ts:224), so no production page has ever
# referenced it. Its POST target `/__manus__/logs` is dev-server middleware and
# does not exist in the Worker either. It was still fetchable on the public
# origin. The dev server reads `client/public/` directly rather than the uploaded
# asset manifest, so excluding it here does not affect `vite dev`.
__manus__/

# Deliberately says nothing about `_headers`. That file is consumed as asset
# metadata and is already absent from the served manifest, so it needs no rule
# here, and naming it would risk excluding it from the metadata pass as well.
#
# `tests/client/assetsIgnore.test.ts` walks the real `client/public` tree and
# checks that these two patterns exclude exactly what is described above and
# nothing else. What it cannot check is that Wrangler's parser agrees with its
# own; that is what a 404 on the deployed URL proves.
